<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Padua</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Patavium, Padova</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Padua (Padova) is a city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veneto">Veneto</a>, north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. As Patavium it was a leading city of the ancient Veneti and a wealthy Roman municipium, the home of the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a>. Its university, founded in 1222, is one of the oldest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, the city came under Venetian rule in 1405, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giotto">Giotto</a>'s frescoes in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scrovegni-chapel">Scrovegni Chapel</a> are among its most famous monuments.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/padua.jpg" alt="Padua" width="320" height="163" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hannelore · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prato_della_Valle.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He had already taken Verona and Vicenza in 1387 and <b>Padua</b> in 1388; he then acquired Pisa in 1399, received the submission of Siena and Perugia in 1399–1400 and took Bologna in 1402.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The mainland Terraferma, conquered between 1339 and 1428, including Treviso, <b>Padua</b>, Vicenza, Verona, Brescia and Bergamo, kept its own statutes and local councils under Venetian rectors.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-venice">Republic of Venice · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Florence, Venice, <b>Padua</b>, Milan and dozens of smaller communes and lordships needed men who could write Latin for contracts, letters, laws and speeches, and the notaries, lawyers and chancery secretaries who did so formed a large lay educated class.</blockquote><a href="/movement/renaissance-humanism">Renaissance humanism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>In the same year Andreas Vesalius, professor at <b>Padua</b>, published On the Fabric of the Human Body, whose detailed woodcuts, based on his own dissections, corrected many of Galen’s errors.</blockquote><a href="/movement/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>By a decree of Pope Boniface VIII, the Sapienza was founded in Rome as a studium for ecclesiastical studies, under more control than the free-standing universities of Bologna and <b>Padua</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-20">20 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Republic rewarded him with a lifetime post at <b>Padua</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-25">25 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
